## Tailwind CSS
{{if eq (index .Traits "frontend-build") "go"}}
The pages are styled with [Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com)
{{.Binaries.tailwindcss.Version}}. `tailwind.css` is the source: it styles the pages' markup and its
classes `row`, `empty`, `problem`, and `name` with `@apply`, and Tailwind
compiles every utility class used in the `.templ` files of
`{{.Slots.web.Dir}}`. The compiled stylesheet, `{{.Slots.web.Dir}}/static/styles.css`,
is committed and embedded in the binary, so building the project needs
neither Node.js nor Tailwind.

After changing `tailwind.css` or the classes in a `.templ` file, rebuild the
stylesheet with `make generate`, or without make:

```sh
go run ./internal/tools/tailwindcss --input tailwind.css --output {{.Slots.web.Dir}}/static/styles.css --minify
```

While you work on the pages, run it with `--watch` instead of `--minify` to
rebuild the stylesheet on every change, and run `make generate` before
committing.

`internal/tools/tailwindcss` runs the standalone Tailwind CSS CLI: on first
use it downloads the release for your platform (about 100 MB), checks its
SHA-256, and caches it in your user cache directory. To use another
`tailwindcss` {{.Binaries.tailwindcss.Version}} binary, for example on musl-based Linux, for which
the release has no build, set `TAILWINDCSS` to its path. The CI workflow
fails when the committed stylesheet is not what Tailwind compiles.
{{- else}}
The frontend is styled with [Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com)
{{.Binaries.tailwindcss.Version}} through its Vite plugin, `@tailwindcss/vite`: the Vite build compiles
every utility class the frontend's sources use. The app's main stylesheet
starts with `@import "tailwindcss";`.
{{- end}}
